diff --git a/README.md b/README.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..2860cdba1857d6be20fa1f16e35dfa9ed2ea7ca7
--- /dev/null
+++ b/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,105 @@
+# Generalized list T-coloring generator (arXiv:1311.0603)
+
+| profile | value |
+|---|---|
+| Track | **B — no-tool compression** |
+| Native domain | combinatorics |
+| Object regime | finite discrete |
+| Computational core | CSP/SAT |
+| Certificate | integer tuple (one natural-number label per vertex) |
+| Intended intuition | invariant: quadratic-residue buckets of public vertex keys are independent sets |
+| Domain essentiality | native; no reduction |
+
+This module instantiates the exact object in Junosza-Szaniawski and Rzążewski, [“An Exact Algorithm for the Generalized List T-Coloring Problem”](https://arxiv.org/abs/1311.0603).  The solver receives a graph, a finite permitted-label list for every vertex, and a forbidden set of absolute label differences for every edge.  It must return one permitted label per vertex while avoiding every forbidden edge difference.  Verification is a direct exact scan of the lists and edges.
+
+## Trust and hardness status
+
+Generation is inverse: balanced hidden classes are sampled first, then labels and only class-crossing edges are drawn.  If local lists are numbered by position, each edge forbids exactly equal positions, so the planted quadratic-bucket labeling is valid by construction.  The generator never solves its output.  All local gates G1–G9(c) pass, including 0 hits in 200,000 structure-aware guesses and four attacks failing on 8/8 shipping seeds.
+
+The Track-B claim is deliberately narrower than an average-case hardness claim.  Section 3, Theorem 1 gives `Solve-GLTC` in `O*((tau+2)^n)` time; storing predecessor transitions recovers a labeling.  The Introduction separately notes `O*(2^n)` for the all-`t(e)={0}` list-coloring case.  Section 3.1 improves the general bound for bounded degree, regular/claw-free, `K_1,d`-free and unit-disk graphs, and graphs with large clique packings.  This family does not claim to evade those algorithms.  Its executable exact DSATUR reference has worst-case `O(q^n(n+m))` cost and solved 8/8 shipping seeds at a median 12,658 nodes, 1,109,063 counted operations, and 0.890 seconds.  After recognizing the key invariant, the compact route takes 256 integer arithmetic operations.
+
+The required four-vendor oracle claim is **not yet established**.  The supplied OpenRouter key returned HTTP 403 “Key limit exceeded” for every bare, structural-hint, and placebo request.  The harness correctly counted none as model failures.  The harness-owned error transcripts are retained; rerun them after restoring OpenRouter quota before submission.
+
+## Worked demo
+
+This is `make_instance(seed=7, **DIFFICULTY["demo"])`, small enough to solve and check by hand:
+
+```text
+key_prime=17, q=3
+vertices (index: key; permitted labels)
+0: 2;  13,14,15
+1: 13; 31,32,33
+2: 12; 19,20,21
+3: 14; 1,2,3
+4: 6;  7,8,9
+5: 4;  25,26,27
+
+edges (u v: forbidden differences)
+0 1: 0,18
+0 2: 0,6
+0 3: 0,12
+0 5: 0,12
+1 3: 0,30
+1 4: 0,24
+2 4: 0,12
+2 5: 0,6
+3 4: 0,6
+```
+
+Square each key modulo 17 and place the residue in one of the three equal-width buckets.  The resulting answer is `<answer>13, 33, 20, 2, 7, 27</answer>`.  `verify(inst, answer)` returns `(True, "ok")`.  Changing the first entry to 14 returns `(False, "edge 1 (0,2) has forbidden difference 6")`.  A person can solve this demo on paper.
+
+## Difficulty presets
+
+| preset | n | q | prime floor | cross-edge rate | status |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|
+| demo | 6 | 3 | 17 | 2/3 | hand example; skipped by hardener |
+| easy | 24 | 4 | 43 | 2/5 | oracle run blocked by HTTP 403 |
+| medium | 40 | 5 | 67 | 1/3 | locally verified |
+| hard | 64 | 6 | 101 | 7/25 | **shipping preset**, locally verified |
+
+Escalation keeps the 64-element witness fixed and enlarges the public-key prime, with only a small move toward 0.30 edge density.  It grows the haystack without lengthening the answer.
+
+## Gate results
+
+| gate | measured result |
+|---|---|
+| G1 | 16/16 planted witnesses verified; JSON round-trips |
+| G2 | 5/5 corruptions rejected with 5 distinct reasons |
+| G3 | tagged prose round-trip recovered all 64 labels |
+| G4 | 0/200,000 valid structure-aware random candidates; space `6^64` |
+| G5 | shipping density 0/200,000; demo exactly 12/729; 32-restart attack failed in 0.027 s; shipping-seed DSATUR 1,850 nodes / 159,716 operations / 0.096 s |
+| G6 | degree residue, left-to-right greedy, 32 greedy restarts, and all affine-key ansätze: 0/8 each; DSATUR reference 8/8 |
+| G7 | doubled `n=128` instance built and verified (2,005 edges) |
+| G8 | 80/80 invariance and 80/80 carried-witness checks; 20/20 unrelated keys distinct |
+| G9(c) | 245 characters, estimated 62 tokens, 64 atoms; 256 intended exact operations |
+
+## Oracle loop and G9 arms
+
+| arm | preset | usable solved/attempts | outcome |
+|---|---|---:|---|
+| bare | easy | 0/0 | four redraws errored HTTP 403; no hardness verdict |
+| structural hint | hard | 0/0 | four redraws errored HTTP 403 |
+| placebo hint | hard | 0/0 | four redraws errored HTTP 403 |
+
+Because there were no usable attempts, hinted minus placebo is undefined in substance (stored as 0.0 only as the module’s zero-attempt sentinel).  No conclusion about hint responsiveness is warranted.  The structural hint names only the invariant: “The hidden color classes are the equal-width buckets of squared vertex keys modulo the displayed prime.”
+
+## Use
+
+```python
+from gen_1311_0603 import DIFFICULTY, make_instance, parse_answer, render, verify
+
+inst = make_instance(seed=123, **DIFFICULTY["hard"])
+prompt = render(inst)
+answer = parse_answer("<answer>" + ",".join(map(str, inst["answer"])) + "</answer>")
+assert verify(inst, answer) == (True, "ok")
+```
+
+From the repository root, emit instances with:
+
+```bash
+bash scripts/emit.sh 1311.0603
+```
+
+## Caveats
+
+The `0/200,000` guess estimate is for a uniform independent choice from every displayed vertex list; it bounds that stated prior, not a solver using graph structure or keys.  The family is deliberately easy for exact DSATUR and for the quadratic-bucket invariant, as Track B requires.  It has not been tested against industrial SAT/CP-SAT, semidefinite relaxations, or a multi-eigenvector spectral clustering implementation; any could make the mechanical route cheaper.  The random graphs usually have large matchings, so the paper’s clique-packing improvement applies even though its worst-case exponential cost remains far beyond hand execution.  Finally, no oracle hardness evidence exists until the OpenRouter quota failure is repaired and all three harness arms are rerun.
